import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "base64.decode strictly reads standard Base64. It checks required = padding, allowed characters, and trailing bits, returning either successful Bytes or a failure reason.",
  taskTitle: "Accept only standard Base64",
  purpose: "Read Zg== from an external text field and recover the original bytes. To avoid alternate spellings and damaged input, accept only the one representation the standard encoder would produce for those bytes.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript calls Uint8Array.fromBase64 with lastChunkHandling: \"strict\" to check incomplete endings and unused bits. It still accepts whitespace, so an additional check requires the result of toBase64() to equal input exactly. try/catch turns decoding exceptions into Rejected.",
  benefit: "Seseragi’s decode includes this standard-format acceptance policy. Its Either<Base64DecodeError, Bytes> result contains either a failure reason in Left or bytes in Right, so the caller handles those two cases. The TypeScript version matches acceptance for the inputs without reproducing the error categories.",
  setup: "base64 aliases std/bytes/base64, and hex aliases std/bytes/hex. On success, decode returns immutable Bytes inside Right. hex.encode makes successful bytes visible by representing each one as two lowercase hexadecimal digits.",
  after: [
    "fn read input: String -> String defines a function from a string named input to a display string. fn introduces the function, : String gives the argument type, and -> String gives the return type. base64.decode input passes the argument after a space.",
    "match branches on Left or Right. The _ in Left _ means the detailed failure reason is not used here. Right content names the successful value content; + joins Accepted:  to hex.encode content.",
    "Zg== becomes one byte with decimal value 102, displayed in hexadecimal as Accepted: 66. The empty string is also accepted. Zm9v becomes three bytes, displayed in hexadecimal as 666f6f and is valid standard Base64 without trailing =. println (read input) displays the string produced by read.",
    "Zg is rejected because it lacks required padding. Whitespace in AA A and Z g==, a misplaced = in AA=A, unused trailing bits in AB==, and the URL-safe input -_8 are also rejected. Ignoring those bits in AB== would let it represent the same bytes as AA==.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Standard Base64 uses A–Z, a–z, 0–9, +, /, and required trailing =. The decoder checks the entire input without removing whitespace or line breaks. Neither omitting necessary = nor adding unnecessary = is allowed.",
    "Base64DecodeError includes InvalidBase64Length carrying the input’s UTF-8 byte length, plus InvalidBase64Digit, InvalidBase64Padding, and NonCanonicalBase64Bits carrying positions. An offset counts input bytes from zero. A length is not an offset.",
    "Length is checked before digits. With multiple defects, the reported error is not necessarily the leftmost one. For example, AA=? produces InvalidBase64Digit at offset 3. Do not treat one result as a list of every defect in the input.",
    "Success gives Bytes rather than text. To recover text, separately call decodeUtf8 from std/text and check that the bytes are valid UTF-8. Base64 decoding does not verify a signature or validate a token.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime has no fromBase64()/toBase64(). With Node/Bun, import { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\". Buffer.from(input, \"base64\") is permissive, so encode its result with toString(\"base64\") and require exact equality with input. This can match the acceptance policy, but it does not reproduce Seseragi’s error categories or check order.",
}
